uuid "f8f2e684-6cd8-5ede-9f70-1a24ada54cb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186520447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186520482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064338 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1045 "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064428 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "073f76c5-241e-56ac-b230-c4ae3778c41c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065250 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065250 #edb/ref 1061 595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35e9e9bc-7d8d-5422-a6d3-4890eb3c5548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065251 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065251 #edb/ref 1061 596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065251 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28874baa-b4bb-54af-b29b-a7cbfc639bc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065251 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065252 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065252 #edb/ref 1061 599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fde7718-a29e-5055-884f-3f58ca08aaec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065252 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065253 #edb/ref 1061 600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065253 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af0ad9db-1ba6-5216-8e5f-5d768427a09e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065253 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1064 "The average rate of change of the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[a,a+h]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as the slope of the secant line that intersects the curve at the points $\\color{blue}P$ and ${\\color{red}Q}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3264f0792cee452453b024a61fb6f58a45290e133d3f84ec1ecf1c14501b4423.png)\n\nThe **instantaneous rate of change** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is the limit of the average rate of change as $h \\to 0$. It is denoted by $f'(a)$ and given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, as $h \\to 0$, the point $\\color{red}Q$ moves along the curve $y=f(x)$ towards the point ${\\color{blue}P}$, and the secant transforms into the line that touches the curve at exactly one point $({\\color{blue}P})$. That line is called the **tangent line** to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $\\color{blue}P$.\n\n![](images/37/3704b7e4f7a7aec6245d3494ef1e59a2b863e74a50bf5c9ecf471eec3789e7e9.png)\n\nThe value of $f'(a)$ is equal to the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1065 322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f6bb245-1f46-5ade-84d4-6fd172b4b227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065365 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1064 "Another way of defining $f'(a)$ is\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nWe arrive at this definition in the same way as before. But this time, we consider the interval $[a,x]$ instead of $[a,a+h]$, and then take the limit of the average rate of change as $x\\to a$.\n\n![](images/b4/b49c6bba1cb82227e7f47b2d1b91eae85ece94fd5251b79173842179cf7946e0.png)\n\nSometimes, this version is a bit easier to work with, so let's see an example of how to apply it. We should always arrive at the same answer regardless of which definition we use." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1065 324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6731f995-584b-5424-a320-c41c218307f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065366 #edb/ref 1067 "An Equivalent Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186520593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1045 "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186520540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519992 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a116c91c-02cc-5bc2-b27d-28406f69f493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520011 #edb/ref 1080 "{f}^{′}(1)=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186520011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218604